<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.13.2: Creating Tables from Verbal Descriptions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00286</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.13.2: Creating Tables from Verbal Descriptions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f7b67e6a-e513-4b92-9980-d90df58fabda</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Creating a Table of Values</title>
<para>For each situation in numbers 1 – 3, create a table of values with at least five columns to represent the situation.</para>

<!--Q#1-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Julio works at a clothing store and makes $14 per hour.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
    <tgroup cols="7"><tbody><row>
        <entry><emphasis>Number of Hours</emphasis></entry><entry>0</entry>
        <entry>1</entry>
        <entry>2</entry>
        <entry>3</entry>
        <entry>4</entry>
        <entry>5</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry><emphasis>Amount Earned</emphasis></entry><entry>0</entry>
        <entry>14</entry>
        <entry>28</entry>
        <entry>42</entry>
        <entry>56</entry>
        <entry>70</entry>
      </row>
    </tbody></tgroup></table>
  
  

</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Jake had cavities at his last dentist visit. The dentist told him to brush his teeth for two minutes each time he brushes.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable unnumbered">
    <tgroup cols="6"><tbody><row>
        <entry><emphasis>Amount Earned</emphasis></entry><entry>1</entry>
        <entry>2</entry>
        <entry>3</entry>
        <entry>4</entry>
        <entry>5</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry><emphasis>Total Number of Minutes Brushed</emphasis></entry><entry>2</entry>
        <entry>4</entry>
        <entry>6</entry>
        <entry>8</entry>
        <entry>10</entry>
      </row>
    </tbody></tgroup></table>
  
  

</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#3-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> For a fundraiser, Celia ran 5 miles on Monday (day 0) and then 3 miles every day after that.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
    <tgroup cols="7"><tbody><row>
        <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry>0</entry>
        <entry>1</entry>
        <entry>2</entry>
        <entry>3</entry>
        <entry>4</entry>
        <entry>5</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry><emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis></entry><entry>5</entry>
        <entry>8</entry>
        <entry>11</entry>
        <entry>14</entry>
        <entry>17</entry>
        <entry>20</entry>
      </row>
    </tbody></tgroup></table>
  
  

</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->

<title>Using a Table of Values to Write Equations</title>
<para>Table of values can be used to write equations. The tables from <emphasis>Creating a Table of Values</emphasis> all represent linear equations.</para>
<para>For 4 - 8, revisit the table from question 1 and write an equation to represent the table of values. Notice that columns for the \(x\) and \(y\) values are added.</para>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <tgroup cols="7"><tbody><row>
      <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry>0</entry>
      <entry>1</entry>
      <entry>2</entry>
      <entry>3</entry>
      <entry>4</entry>
      <entry>5</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis></entry><entry>0</entry>
      <entry>14</entry>
      <entry>28</entry>
      <entry>42</entry>
      <entry>56</entry>
      <entry>70</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody></tgroup></table>

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="_7a5095fe-59e3-4bcf-9f11-a5bdfa8e1c46" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the starting value (when \(x = 0\), what does \(y\) equal)?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(y = 0\)</para><para>The correct answer is \(y = 0\).</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Q#5-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> How would you describe this value on the graph?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(y\)-intercept</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#6-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What is the rate of change, or the change between the \(y\)-values over the change between the \(x\)-values? 
        <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text"><iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/nZiRu4PpXDo?si=5Rg6NDyzCgjfNHwO;&amp;rel=0"/></media></para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(\frac{14}{1}\) or 14</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#7-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> How would you describe this value on the graph?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>slope</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#8-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Using the information in questions 4 – 7, what would the equation be for the values represented by the table? Use slope-intercept form.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(y= 14x +0\) or \(y = 14x\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#9-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write an equation from the table of values created in question 2 of <emphasis>Creating a Table of Values</emphasis>.</para>
    <para>Hint: (Work backward to find the value where \(x = 0\) using the pattern set in the table of values).</para>
    
    <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
      <tgroup cols="7"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry>0</entry>
          <entry>1</entry>
          <entry>2</entry>
          <entry>3</entry>
          <entry>4</entry>
          <entry>5</entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis></entry><entry>?</entry>
          <entry>2</entry>
          <entry>4</entry>
          <entry>6</entry>
          <entry>8</entry>
          <entry>10</entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    
  
  </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(y = 2x\) (note: subtract the top row by 1 and the bottom row by 2 to go back to where \(x = 0\) for the \(y\)-intercept).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#10-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write an equation from the table of values created in question 3 of <emphasis>Creating a Table of Values</emphasis></para>
    <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
      <tgroup cols="7"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry>0</entry>
          <entry>1</entry>
          <entry>2</entry>
          <entry>3</entry>
          <entry>4</entry>
          <entry>5</entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis></entry><entry>?</entry>
          <entry>2</entry>
          <entry>4</entry>
          <entry>6</entry>
          <entry>8</entry>
          <entry>10</entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    
  
  </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(y = 3x +5\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<title>Video: Writing an Equation from a Table of Values</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about writing equations from a table of values.</para>
<media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text"><iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/nZiRu4PpXDo?si=OEfKjtDOzQiG_BR2;&amp;rel=0"/></media><title>Create Your Own Situation</title>
<para>Think of your own situation that could be modeled by a linear equation. Create a table of values that represents that situation. Write your problem down then switch with a partner. Make a table of values for each other’s problems. Then, check your work together.</para>
<para>Next, plot the points of each table of values in a coordinate plane. 
  Answer the questions after discussing with your partner:</para>

<!--Q#1-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Describe the scenario created by your partner.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Answers vary.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->



<!--Q#2-->
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Use Desmos to graph the table of values to represent your partner’s scenario.</item>
</list>
<para>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the equation that represents this scenario using the Desmos tool below.</para>
<iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <!--QUESTION IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
  
  <para>When you have finished graphing, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>Answers vary.</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#3-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Does your partner’s data model a linear relationship or not? Describe how you determined your answer.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>The points form a line. This happens because the same number was added to each value in the top row and the same number was added to each value in the bottom row of the tables.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  
  <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  
  </problem></exercise>
</example><!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
    
    <para> Kylie pays $20 to rent a scooter plus $0.40 per mile she drives the scooter. Create a table of values to represent Kylie’s situation starting with \(x=0\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
      <tgroup cols="6"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>0</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>1</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>2</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>3</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>4</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>5</emphasis></entry></row>
      <row>
          <entry>20</entry>
          <entry>20.40</entry>
          <entry>20.80</entry>
          <entry>21.20</entry>
          <entry>21.60</entry>
          <entry>22.10</entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>Use the table to determine how much she has to pay after 5 miles.
     </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Kylie would pay $22.10.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->
</section>
